// std/rand.momo - a 16-bit xorshift generator.
//
// Good enough for a game, not for anything else. Period 65535, and **every bit is
// as good as every other** - so a caller takes the value as it comes, and nothing
// has to know which bits are safe to use.
//
// **It costs roughly twice what a power-of-two LCG would**, in the region of 400
// cycles on an 8086. Worth paying a few times a frame, worth knowing before
// calling it per pixel: a 64000-pixel fill notices and nothing else will.
//
// Sixteen bits of state is the ceiling here - consecutive pairs cover about half
// the 65536 possible, which is fine for dice, loot and combat. **Procedural noise
// wants something else entirely**, a positional hash that is a pure function of
// coordinates rather than a stream. That is a different primitive, not a better
// version of this one, so it does not belong in this file.

// `local`, so the state is reachable only from this file - otherwise a program
// could write `randomSeed = 0` directly and walk straight past the guard below.
local u16 randomSeed = 42

// Zero is a fixed point: xorshift would return it forever. Mapped rather than
// rejected, so seeding can never silently kill the generator.
sub seedRandom(u16 s) => randomSeed = s == 0 ? 1 : s

// Shifts of 7, 9, 8 give the full 65535-state cycle. Arithmetic is 16-bit
// throughout, so the left shifts truncate exactly as the algorithm wants.
u16 nextRandom() {
  randomSeed = randomSeed ^ (randomSeed << 7)
  randomSeed = randomSeed ^ (randomSeed >> 9)
  randomSeed = randomSeed ^ (randomSeed << 8)
  return randomSeed
}

// The low bits carry the full period, so the modulo takes the value as it comes.
// `n` must not be zero: an 8086 divide by zero raises INT 0.
u16 randomBelow(u16 n) => nextRandom() % n

// Inclusive on both ends.
u16 randomBetween(u16 low, u16 high) => low + randomBelow(high - low + 1)
